## Problem

Design a car rental system such as Zoomcar or Hertz. A customer searches for a car type at a branch for some dates, reserves a car, picks it up, and returns it later. The branch needs time to clean a car between customers.

The key question hides in the dates: two customers must never be promised the same car for overlapping times, and a late return must be charged fairly.

## Requirements

- Search available vehicles by branch, vehicle type (hatchback, sedan, SUV) and time range.
- Reserve a specific vehicle for a time range and get a price quote.
- Pick up the car at the start time and return it later; record odometer and fuel.
- Charge ₹1,800 for each started 24 hours of the booking and ₹200 for each started late hour.
- Keep one hour after every rental for cleaning before the car can go out again.
- Cancel a reservation before pickup.

Out of scope: one-way rentals between branches, insurance and add-ons, driver licence verification, payment gateway details.

## 1. Clarify the scope

| Question to ask | Answer we choose | Why it matters |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Do we reserve a car type or a specific car? | A specific car, chosen by the system. | Availability becomes a per-car calendar check. |
| Same branch for pickup and return? | Yes. | One-way rentals need cars to move between branches; skip it. |
| What happens between two rentals? | A one-hour cleaning buffer. | The buffer must be part of the overlap check. |
| How are late returns handled? | Charge per started late hour. | Pricing needs the actual return time, not just the booked one. |
| How many staff or apps reserve at once? | Several at the same time. | Reserving needs protection against two people taking the same slot. |

A 45-minute plan:

| Minutes | What you do |
| --- | --- |
| 0–5 | Requirements and the questions above |
| 5–15 | Classes and statuses |
| 15–25 | Availability check (interval overlap) |
| 25–35 | Reserve, pickup, return and pricing code |
| 35–45 | Concurrency, patterns and follow-ups |

## 2. Core classes

| Class | What it represents | Key fields |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Branch` | A rental location | id, name, city, vehicles |
| `Vehicle` | One physical car | id ("CAR17"), registration, `VehicleType`, `VehicleStatus` |
| `Customer` | The person renting | id, name, licenceNumber |
| `Reservation` | A booking of one vehicle for one time range | id, customer, vehicle, start, end, `ReservationStatus`, pickedUpAt, returnedAt |
| `Invoice` | What the customer owes | reservationId, baseAmount, lateFee, total |
| `RentalService` | Coordinates search, reserve, pickup, return, cancel | branches, reservations, pricing |
| `PricingStrategy` | Calculates base and late charges | rates per vehicle type |

```java
enum VehicleType { HATCHBACK, SEDAN, SUV }
enum VehicleStatus { AVAILABLE, RENTED, IN_MAINTENANCE }
enum ReservationStatus { CONFIRMED, PICKED_UP, RETURNED, CANCELLED }
```

[![Class diagram: Branch has Vehicles; Reservation links a Customer to one Vehicle for a start and end time and produces an Invoice; RentalService searches, reserves, picks up, returns and cancels, and uses a PricingStrategy.](learning-figures/lld-6-classes.svg)](learning-figures/lld-6-classes.svg)

**Do we need `Car`, `Suv` and `Hatchback` subclasses?** Only if they behave differently. Here they differ only by data (type and rate), so an enum is enough. If SUVs later needed a special deposit rule, that rule would go into a pricing or deposit strategy rather than a subclass.

**Why keep `VehicleStatus` and reservations separate?** The status says what the car is doing *now* (on the road, in maintenance). Reservations say what is promised for the *future*. A car can be AVAILABLE today and fully booked next weekend.

[![Selected design: RentalService searches a Branch's vehicles, creates Reservations that book a vehicle's time slot, uses a PricingStrategy and produces an Invoice on return.](designs/lld-6.svg)](designs/lld-6.svg)

## 3. The availability algorithm: an interval check

Example: customer A books CAR17 from 09:00 to 11:00. With one hour of cleaning, the car is **blocked from 09:00 to 12:00**.

- Customer B asks for 11:00–14:00: it overlaps the blocked time, so CAR17 is not available.
- Customer B asks for 12:00–15:00: it starts exactly when the block ends, so CAR17 is available.

[![Timeline: A's rental from 09:00 to 11:00 plus cleaning until 12:00 blocks the car; a request starting at 11:00 overlaps and is rejected, a request starting at 12:00 is allowed.](learning-figures/lld-6-intervals.svg)](learning-figures/lld-6-intervals.svg)

Two time ranges `[start, end)` overlap when **each starts before the other ends**:

```java
class Reservation {
    static final Duration CLEANING = Duration.ofHours(1);
    // id, customer, vehicle, start, end, status, pickedUpAt, returnedAt ...

    Instant blockedUntil() {
        return end.plus(CLEANING);
    }

    boolean isActive() {
        return status == ReservationStatus.CONFIRMED || status == ReservationStatus.PICKED_UP;
    }

    boolean overlaps(Instant otherStart, Instant otherEnd) {
        Instant otherBlockedUntil = otherEnd.plus(CLEANING);
        return start.isBefore(otherBlockedUntil) && otherStart.isBefore(blockedUntil());
    }
}
```

Three details interviewers check:

- **Strict `isBefore`, not `<=`.** A 12:00 start touching a 12:00 end is allowed.
- **Both conditions.** Checking only "does the new start fall inside the old booking?" misses a new booking that completely surrounds an old one.
- **The buffer on both sides.** The new booking also needs its own cleaning time before the next existing booking.

## 4. Reserve, pick up and return

```java
public class RentalService {
    private final Map<String, List<Reservation>> reservationsByVehicle = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final PricingStrategy pricing;
    private final Clock clock;

    public List<Vehicle> search(Branch branch, VehicleType type, Instant start, Instant end) {
        return branch.getVehicles().stream()
                .filter(v -> v.getType() == type && v.getStatus() != VehicleStatus.IN_MAINTENANCE)
                .filter(v -> isFree(v, start, end))
                .toList();
    }

    public Reservation reserve(Customer customer, Vehicle vehicle, Instant start, Instant end) {
        if (!start.isBefore(end) || start.isBefore(clock.instant())) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid time range");
        }
        synchronized (vehicle) {                     // check and insert together
            if (!isFree(vehicle, start, end)) {
                throw new VehicleUnavailableException(vehicle.getId());
            }
            Reservation r = new Reservation(UUID.randomUUID().toString(), customer, vehicle, start, end);
            reservationsByVehicle.computeIfAbsent(vehicle.getId(), id -> new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>()).add(r);
            return r;
        }
    }

    private boolean isFree(Vehicle vehicle, Instant start, Instant end) {
        return reservationsByVehicle.getOrDefault(vehicle.getId(), List.of()).stream()
                .filter(Reservation::isActive)
                .noneMatch(r -> r.overlaps(start, end));
    }
}
```

The search result is a suggestion. Between search and reserve another customer may take the car, so `reserve` checks again inside `synchronized (vehicle)`. Two requests for **different** cars never wait for each other.

Pickup and return move the reservation through its statuses:

```java
public void pickup(Reservation r, int odometerKm) {
    synchronized (r.getVehicle()) {
        if (r.getStatus() != ReservationStatus.CONFIRMED) throw new IllegalStateException("Not pickable");
        if (r.getVehicle().getStatus() != VehicleStatus.AVAILABLE) throw new VehicleNotReadyException();
        r.markPickedUp(clock.instant(), odometerKm);
        r.getVehicle().setStatus(VehicleStatus.RENTED);
    }
}

public Invoice returnVehicle(Reservation r, int odometerKm, boolean damaged) {
    synchronized (r.getVehicle()) {
        if (r.getStatus() != ReservationStatus.PICKED_UP) throw new IllegalStateException("Not on rent");
        Instant now = clock.instant();
        r.markReturned(now, odometerKm);
        r.getVehicle().setStatus(damaged ? VehicleStatus.IN_MAINTENANCE : VehicleStatus.AVAILABLE);
        return pricing.invoice(r, now);
    }
}
```

### Pricing

```java
interface PricingStrategy {
    Invoice invoice(Reservation r, Instant returnedAt);
}

class DailyRatePricing implements PricingStrategy {
    private static final long DAY_RATE_PAISE = 1_80_000;   // ₹1,800
    private static final long LATE_HOUR_PAISE = 20_000;    // ₹200

    public Invoice invoice(Reservation r, Instant returnedAt) {
        long days = startedUnits(Duration.between(r.getStart(), r.getEnd()), Duration.ofDays(1));
        Duration late = returnedAt.isAfter(r.getEnd()) ? Duration.between(r.getEnd(), returnedAt) : Duration.ZERO;
        long lateHours = startedUnits(late, Duration.ofHours(1));
        long base = days * DAY_RATE_PAISE, lateFee = lateHours * LATE_HOUR_PAISE;
        return new Invoice(r.getId(), base, lateFee, base + lateFee);
    }

    static long startedUnits(Duration d, Duration unit) {   // 25 hours -> 2 days
        long whole = d.dividedBy(unit);
        return d.equals(unit.multipliedBy(whole)) ? whole : whole + 1;
    }
}
```

Worked example: A booked 09:00–11:00 (one started day, ₹1,800) and returned at 12:10 (70 minutes late, two started hours, ₹400). Total ₹2,200. Money is kept in paise as a `long` so rounding never loses a rupee.

## 5. Design patterns and extensibility

| Pattern | Where | Why it helps |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Strategy** | `PricingStrategy` | Weekend rates, per-km pricing or memberships without touching `RentalService`. |
| **Factory** (optional) | Creating vehicles from a type | Useful if vehicle creation grows; not needed for an enum. |
| **Observer** | Reservation created / car returned late | Email, SMS or "your car may be late" alerts without changing core code. |

Extensions to mention:

- *Add-ons* (child seat, GPS): a list of `AddOn` items on the reservation, each with its own price.
- *Different cleaning times*: move `CLEANING` onto `VehicleType` (SUVs may need longer).
- *Faster search for big fleets*: keep each car's reservations sorted by start time, or use an interval tree, instead of scanning all.

## 6. Interviewer follow-ups

**A is late and B's pickup time arrives. What now?** B's reservation still exists, but CAR17 is RENTED, so pickup fails with "vehicle not ready". The system should offer B another available car of the same type (a new reserve call for that car). If none exists, cancel B's booking free of charge – the delay is not B's fault. A is charged the late fee.

**How do you prevent two staff members reserving the same car for the same time?** Check and insert in one critical section per vehicle. With a database, lock that vehicle's row (`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`) while checking overlaps and inserting, or use a database exclusion constraint on the time range (PostgreSQL supports this). Both make the database the single referee.

**Why reserve a specific car and not just "an SUV"?** It makes availability a simple per-car check. Promising only a type needs capacity counting across overlapping times, which is harder; you can still let customers *search* by type.

**What if the car comes back damaged?** Mark it IN_MAINTENANCE so search skips it, and charge damage through a separate inspection step. Future reservations on that car need to be moved to another car.

**How do you handle cancellation?** Only a CONFIRMED reservation can be cancelled. Set it to CANCELLED so it stops blocking the calendar. A `CancellationPolicy` (another strategy) decides the fee, for example free before the start time.

**Where do time zones fit?** Store times as `Instant` (UTC). Convert to the branch's time zone only when displaying.

## 7. Going deeper (optional)

The full reference design adds production details you can bring up in senior rounds:

- **Quotes**: the server stores a quote (car, dates, price, valid for 5 minutes). The customer confirms the quote ID, so the price cannot change between screens.
- **Retry safety**: a confirmation retried with the same request ID returns the same reservation instead of booking twice.
- **Old events**: a delayed "return" message for an old rental must not free a car that is now on a newer rental. Each event names the reservation it belongs to.
- **Operator vs customer fault**: if the car is not ready because of a late return, the next customer is never charged a no-show fee.

[![A's late return leaves CAR17 with R1 when B arrives; the return at 12:10 moves readiness to 13:10, B's reservation stays visible and is not charged a no-show fee.](learning-figures/lld-6-readiness.svg)](learning-figures/lld-6-readiness.svg)

[Open the rental timeline walkthrough](learning-labs/rental.html), or read the runnable Java: [Rental.java](reference-code/learning/kb/rental/Rental.java), [RentalDemo.java](reference-code/learning/kb/rental/RentalDemo.java) and [RentalChecks.java](reference-code/learning/kb/rental/RentalChecks.java).

## 8. Closing answer

"I model Branch, Vehicle, Customer, Reservation and Invoice, with a `RentalService` coordinating search, reserve, pickup, return and cancel. Vehicle status describes the car now; reservations describe the future. Availability is an interval-overlap check on half-open ranges that include a one-hour cleaning buffer. Reserving re-checks and inserts under a per-vehicle lock, or a row lock in the database, so two customers can never get the same slot. Pricing is a strategy that charges per started day plus per started late hour, in paise. A late return blocks the next pickup, so the system offers another car or a free cancellation."

## Problem

You are adding AI features to an existing Spring Boot application. Your lead asks: *"What is Spring AI, how do we wire it in, and how do we avoid locking ourselves to one AI provider?"*

Build the smallest useful example – a `POST /api/explain` endpoint that explains a Java concept – and explain the Spring AI building blocks and how Spring Boot wires them together.

## Requirements

- Explain what Spring AI is and why a Java team would use it.
- Add the dependencies correctly (BOM and starter) and configure a provider.
- Explain the main abstractions: `ChatModel`, `ChatClient`, `EmbeddingModel`, `Prompt`.
- Build an endpoint with a service that uses `ChatClient`.
- Switch providers (cloud vs local Ollama) with configuration only.
- Test without calling a real model.

## 1. The idea in plain words

**Spring AI** is to AI models what Spring Data is to databases: one familiar, Spring-style API in front of many providers – OpenAI, Anthropic, Azure OpenAI, Google Gemini, Amazon Bedrock, Ollama and more.

You get:

- **Portable interfaces** (`ChatModel`, `EmbeddingModel`) with one implementation per provider.
- **Auto-configuration**: add a starter and a few properties, and Spring Boot creates the beans.
- A fluent **`ChatClient`** for building prompts, plus structured output, chat memory, RAG (`VectorStore`), tool calling and observability.

**What interviewers usually ask:** what `ChatModel` and `ChatClient` are and how they differ; how auto-configuration picks a model; how to switch providers; how to test AI code.

## 2. Add it to a Spring Boot project

Use the **BOM** (bill of materials) so all Spring AI modules have matching versions, then add **one starter per provider**:

```xml
<dependencyManagement>
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.ai</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-ai-bom</artifactId>
      <version>1.1.8</version>
      <type>pom</type>
      <scope>import</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</dependencyManagement>

<dependencies>
  <dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
  </dependency>
  <dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.ai</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-ai-starter-model-openai</artifactId>   <!-- or ...-model-ollama -->
  </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

```properties
spring.ai.openai.api-key=${OPENAI_API_KEY}
spring.ai.openai.chat.options.model=gpt-4o-mini
spring.ai.openai.chat.options.temperature=0.3
```

Keep API keys in environment variables or a secrets manager – never in Git.

**What happens at startup** – this is **auto-configuration**:

1. Spring Boot sees the OpenAI starter on the classpath and the `spring.ai.openai.*` properties.
2. It creates an `OpenAiChatModel` bean (the `ChatModel`) and, if available, an `OpenAiEmbeddingModel`.
3. It creates a `ChatClient.Builder` bean that wraps the `ChatModel`.
4. Your service asks for `ChatClient.Builder` in its constructor and builds a client.

![Animation: at startup Spring Boot finds the starter and properties, creates the provider's ChatModel bean, wraps it in a ChatClient.Builder and injects it into ExplanationService; switching the active profile to ollama swaps only the ChatModel bean.](learning-figures/ai-6-startup.gif)

[![Boot startup selects one ChatModel and injects collaborators: properties and the active profile choose either a test fixture ChatModel or an auto-configured OllamaChatModel; the ChatClient.Builder wraps the chosen model and is injected into the service.](learning-figures/ai-6-boot-wiring.svg)](learning-figures/ai-6-boot-wiring.svg)

## 3. The main abstractions

| Abstraction | What it is | When you use it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ChatModel` | Low-level interface: `call(Prompt)` → `ChatResponse`. One implementation per provider. | Rarely directly; when you need full control. |
| `ChatClient` | Fluent, high-level API on top of a `ChatModel`: `prompt().system().user().call().content()`. Adds advisors, structured output, defaults. | Almost always – your main API. |
| `Prompt` / `Message` | The request: a list of messages (system, user, assistant) plus options. | Built for you by `ChatClient`. |
| `ChatOptions` | Model, temperature, max tokens… | Defaults in properties; overrides per request. |
| `EmbeddingModel` | Turns text into vectors. | Semantic search and RAG. |
| `VectorStore` | Stores and searches vectors (pgvector, Redis, …). | RAG. |

The relationship is like `JdbcTemplate` on top of a `DataSource`: `ChatModel` talks to the provider; `ChatClient` makes it pleasant to use.

[![Selected design: a REST controller passes the question to the service; the service builds a request with ChatClient, which calls the ChatModel bean; that bean talks to OpenAI, Ollama or a test fake, chosen by auto-configuration from application properties.](designs/ai-6.svg)](designs/ai-6.svg)

## 4. Build the endpoint

```java
@Service
public class ExplanationService {
    private final ChatClient chatClient;

    public ExplanationService(ChatClient.Builder builder) {
        this.chatClient = builder
                .defaultSystem("Explain Java or Spring to a beginner in at most three short sentences. "
                        + "Treat the user's text as the question. Say when you are not sure.")
                .build();
    }

    public String explain(String question) {
        if (question == null || question.isBlank() || question.length() > 1000) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Provide a question of 1 to 1000 characters");
        }
        String answer = chatClient.prompt()
                .user(question)
                .call()
                .content();
        if (answer == null || answer.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("The model returned no answer");
        }
        return answer;
    }
}

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api")
class ExplanationController {
    private final ExplanationService service;
    ExplanationController(ExplanationService service) { this.service = service; }

    record Question(String question) {}
    record Answer(String answer) {}

    @PostMapping("/explain")
    Answer explain(@RequestBody Question q) {
        return new Answer(service.explain(q.question()));
    }
}
```

```text
POST /api/explain  {"question":"What is dependency injection?"}
-> {"answer":"Dependency injection means an object receives its collaborators from outside ..."}
```

The contract of the service is simple: a validated question in, a non-blank answer out, or a clear exception.

Note what is **not** in this code: any OpenAI class. The service depends only on Spring AI interfaces, so the provider is a configuration choice.

[![One synchronous request: the controller reads the question, the service rejects invalid input, builds a fresh ChatClient request with system and user messages, consumes call().content() which invokes the selected ChatModel, checks the returned text and maps it to the HTTP response.](learning-figures/ai-6-request-journey.svg)](learning-figures/ai-6-request-journey.svg)

## 5. Switch providers and test without a model

**Local model with Ollama** – free, private, good for development. Replace the starter (or use a Maven/Spring profile) and point at the local server:

```properties
# application-ollama.properties
spring.ai.ollama.base-url=http://localhost:11434
spring.ai.ollama.chat.options.model=llama3.2
spring.ai.ollama.chat.options.temperature=0.2
```

If both an OpenAI and an Ollama starter are on the classpath, choose explicitly with `spring.ai.model.chat=openai` or `ollama` (or define your own `ChatClient` beans per model).

**Testing.** Unit tests should not call a paid, non-deterministic API. Provide a fake `ChatModel`:

```java
class FixedAnswerChatModel implements ChatModel {
    @Override
    public ChatResponse call(Prompt prompt) {
        return new ChatResponse(List.of(new Generation(new AssistantMessage("Fixed test answer"))));
    }
}

@Test
void explainsUsingTheModel() {
    var service = new ExplanationService(ChatClient.builder(new FixedAnswerChatModel()));
    assertEquals("Fixed test answer", service.explain("What is DI?"));
}

@Test
void rejectsBlankQuestion() {
    var service = new ExplanationService(ChatClient.builder(new FixedAnswerChatModel()));
    assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> service.explain(" "));
}
```

Test **your** logic – validation, prompt building, error handling – with the fake. Check model quality separately with a small evaluation set against the real model.

**When something breaks, check in this order:** is the starter on the classpath → are the properties and API key set → is the right profile active → can the app reach the provider (network, proxy) → is the model name valid → did the call time out.

## 6. Interviewer follow-ups

**`ChatModel` vs `ChatClient`?** `ChatModel` is the low-level, provider-specific implementation of a common interface. `ChatClient` is the fluent API built on it, adding system defaults, advisors (memory, RAG, logging), structured output and streaming.

**How do you use two different models in one app?** Create two `ChatClient` beans, each built from a different `ChatModel` (for example a cheap model for classification and a stronger one for answers), and inject them with qualifiers.

**Is Spring AI only for OpenAI?** No – it supports many providers through the same interfaces, including local models via Ollama.

**Should the controller call `ChatClient` directly?** Better in a service: validation, prompts and error handling stay testable and reusable.

**How do you handle provider errors?** Spring AI has built-in retries (`spring.ai.retry.*`) for transient errors; add timeouts, map failures to clear HTTP errors (e.g. 503), and log usage.

**How do you observe AI calls?** Spring AI integrates with Micrometer: it records latency and token usage metrics and traces for model calls.

## 7. Going deeper (optional)

The detailed version follows auto-configuration step by step (BOM vs starter, conditional bean creation, profiles), shows exactly which boundary to inspect first when something breaks, and ships a complete runnable project that works offline with a fixture model and against a local Ollama model with one profile switch.

[Open the Spring wiring lab](learning-labs/spring-fundamentals.html), download the [complete project](reference-code/learning/spring-fundamentals.zip), or read its [focused tests](reference-code/learning/spring-fundamentals/src/test/java/kb/springfundamentals/FundamentalsTest.java). See the [Spring AI reference documentation](https://docs.spring.io/spring-ai/reference/) for supported providers.

## 8. Closing answer

"Spring AI gives Java teams one Spring-style API over many AI providers. I import the Spring AI BOM and add one provider starter; auto-configuration creates the provider's `ChatModel` and a `ChatClient.Builder` from properties. My service injects the builder, sets a default system prompt and calls `chatClient.prompt().user(question).call().content()`. Because the code depends only on Spring AI interfaces, switching from OpenAI to a local Ollama model is a configuration change. `ChatModel` is the low-level provider interface, `ChatClient` the fluent API with advisors and structured output, and `EmbeddingModel` and `VectorStore` support RAG. I unit-test with a fake `ChatModel` and evaluate real model quality separately."
